# Internationalization Guidelines

## Scope / Flexibility Notice
These guidelines define patterns for implementing and maintaining internationalization across the Flutter Chat Application. They apply to all modules and features of the application where user-facing text is displayed. Compliance is mandatory for:
- All UI components across all supported platforms (mobile, web, desktop)
- All error messages and notifications
- All dynamic content including dates, numbers, and currency values
- All RTL/LTR layout considerations

Limited exceptions may be made for:
- Debug-only text that users never see
- Specialized content like code snippets or fixed format data
- Third-party widget libraries that cannot be modified

## Best Practices
- Use ARB files for all translations (instead of JSON files)
- Never hardcode user-visible strings
- Always provide descriptive comments for translators in ARB files
- Use the `context.l10n` extension for accessing translations
- Implement proper pluralization with ICU message syntax
- Structure translation keys in a logical hierarchy
- Test UI with various language string lengths
- Use the LocaleCubit for managing language state
- Support bidirectionality for languages like Arabic, Hebrew
- Avoid string concatenation for forming sentences
- Use string templates with placeholders for dynamic content
- Implement flexible UI layouts that accommodate text expansion
- Handle gracefully missing translations with fallbacks
- Use dedicated widgets for language selection and indication
- Make date/time formats consistent across the application
- Create separate implementations for platform-specific UI elements

## File / Project Structure Guidelines
- Place all ARB files in `lib/l10n/` directory
- Name ARB files according to language code: `app_en.arb`, `app_vi.arb`, etc.
- Define supported locales in `lib/l10n/l10n.dart`
- Implement locale management Cubit in `lib/presentation/blocs/locale/`
- Store locale persistence logic in `lib/core/services/localization_service.dart`
- Place language selection UI components in `lib/presentation/widgets/settings/`
- Keep generated localization files in `.dart_tool/flutter_gen/` directory
- Register LocaleCubit and LocalizationService in DI container
- Document localization procedures in `lib/l10n/README.md`
- Store detailed guidelines in `docs/internationalization_best_practices.md`
- Include example usage in code documentation

## Coding & Styling Conventions
- Use camelCase for translation keys
- Group related keys with dot notation: `category.subcategory.keyName`
- Format ARB files with consistent indentation
- Always include description metadata for each key
- Provide placeholder examples in ARB metadata
- Use standard ICU message syntax for parameters and plurals
- Avoid string concatenation; use parameterized messages instead
- Create extension methods for commonly used formatting patterns
- Add trailing commas to improve ARB file diffs
- Define semantic grouping with empty line separators between logical sections
- Follow consistent naming conventions for locale-related classes and variables
- Use proper annotation types for generated code (e.g., `@immutable` for state classes)

## Implementation Example
```dart
// Good: Using localization extension with proper context
Text(context.l10n.chatMessageCount(count)),

// Bad: Hardcoded string
Text("$count messages"),

// Good: Using ICU parameter with placeholders
"userPostedMessage": "{username} posted: {message}",
"@userPostedMessage": {
  "description": "Shows who posted a message and what they wrote",
  "placeholders": {
    "username": {
      "type": "String",
      "example": "John"
    },
    "message": {
      "type": "String",
      "example": "Hello everyone!"
    }
  }
}

// Good: Complete widget implementation with localization
class LocalizedGreeting extends StatelessWidget {
  final String username;
  
  const LocalizedGreeting({super.key, required this.username});
  
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Text(
      context.l10n.greeting(username),
      style: Theme.of(context).textTheme.bodyLarge,
    );
  }
}
```

## Parameter Handling
- Use named placeholders: `"hello": "Hello, {name}"`
- Document all placeholders in metadata
- Format parameters appropriately: dates, numbers, etc.
- Use selective parameters for gender/context variations
- Example:
  ```json
  "greeting": "Hello, {name}",
  "@greeting": {
    "description": "Greeting with user's name",
    "placeholders": {
      "name": {
        "type": "String",
        "example": "John"
      }
    }
  }
  ```

## Pluralization
- Always handle quantity variations with plurals
- Implement at least three forms: zero, one, and other
- Example:
  ```json
  "messageCount": "{count, plural, =0{No messages} =1{1 message} other{{count} messages}}",
  "@messageCount": {
    "description": "Number of messages with pluralization",
    "placeholders": {
      "count": {
        "type": "int",
        "format": "compact"
      }
    }
  }
  ```

## Date, Time and Number Formatting
- Use ICU date/time formats for consistent localization
- Define standard date/time formats in the L10n helper class
- Use appropriate number formats for different locales
- Examples:
  ```json
  "lastUpdateTime": "Last updated: {time}",
  "@lastUpdateTime": {
    "description": "Time when content was last updated",
    "placeholders": {
      "time": {
        "type": "DateTime", 
        "format": "Hm"
      }
    }
  }
  ```

## Language Selection Components
- Use `LanguageSelectorScreen` for full language selection UI
- Use `LanguageToggleButton` for quick language switching
- Use `LanguageIndicator` for displaying current language
- Always provide visual confirmation when language changes
- Support both system default and specific language selection
- Example:
  ```dart
  // Change to a specific language
  context.read<LocaleCubit>().changeLocale(const Locale('vi'));
  
  // Reset to system default
  context.read<LocaleCubit>().changeLocale(null);
  
  // Toggle between available languages
  context.read<LocaleCubit>().toggleLocale();
  ```

## RTL Support Guidelines
- Use start/end instead of left/right for positioning
- Implement mirrored layouts for RTL languages
- Test layouts in both RTL and LTR modes
- Consider using `Directionality` widget for local direction changes
- Example:
  ```dart
  // Good: Using directional-aware properties
  Padding(
    padding: const EdgeInsets.only(start: 16, end: 8),
    child: Text(context.l10n.greeting),
  ),
  
  // Bad: Using left/right which don't adapt to RTL
  Padding(
    padding: const EdgeInsets.only(left: 16, right: 8),
    child: Text("Hello"),
  ),
  ```

## Performance & Optimization Tips
- Use the `generate: true` flag in pubspec.yaml
- Access translations via context extension for optimal performance
- Lazy-load translations when appropriate
- Optimize image assets and other resources for different locales
- Consider text length variations in UI design
- Use conditional widgets for dramatically different layouts between languages
- Avoid rebuilding widgets unnecessarily due to locale changes
- Use const constructors where possible for better performance
- Cache frequently used formatted values (dates, numbers) to avoid repeated formatting
- Pre-load critical translations during app initialization

## Testing & Validation Guidelines
- Test UI in all supported languages
- Verify text doesn't overflow containers
- Ensure all strings are translated
- Check parameter substitution is correct
- Verify pluralization works for all quantity ranges
- Test RTL layout if supporting RTL languages
- Simulate missing translations to verify fallbacks
- Validate translation bundle size doesn't grow excessively
- Write tests that validate all translations are loaded correctly
- Create automated checks for missing translations
- Test language switching at runtime
- Verify proper handling of system locale changes

